        "content": "<p>The docstring of lemma <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=hom_coe_pow#doc\">docs#hom_coe_pow</a> says,</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"sd\">/-- An auxiliary lemma that can be used to prove `⇑(f ^ n) = ⇑f^[n]`. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">to_additive</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hom_coe_pow</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Monoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">id</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hmul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>However I would argue that this lemma isn't useful to do this, because when writing a <code>Monoid</code> instance for a <code>FunLike</code> really we would like the <code>npow</code> field of the <code>Monoid</code> instance to be so that this lemma holds definitionally (instead of being something like <code>npowRec</code>), and this lemma only works when we already have a <code>Monoid</code> instance, but we would like to use it when defining the instance, for example, to set <code>npow n f := (npowRec n f).copy (f^[n]) ⋯</code>.</p>\n<p>This tricky situation where you would like to use some algebraic structure to fix its own defeqs also comes up in other places. For example, maybe I have that <code>Foo R</code> is a ring when <code>R</code> is, and <code>Foo S</code> is an <code>R</code>-algebra when <code>S</code> is. Then I might want to set <code>natCast n := algebraMap ℕ (Foo R) n</code> (since <code>R</code> is a <code>ℕ</code>-algebra so is <code>Foo R</code>), but this runs into the problem that <code>natCast</code> is a field of <code>Ring</code> and <code>algebraMap</code> requires the <code>Ring (Foo R)</code> to already be constructed.</p>",
